  "query_preview": "..A strong area of high pressure is centered over our\narea this afternoon, bringing hot temperatures from 5 to 10\ndegrees above average for this time of year. The flow around the\nridge is bringing drier air to areas north of I-40 where surface \ndewpoints have fallen to the 30s/low 40s. Sufficient moisture \nremains along/south of I-40 for isolated to scattered showers and\nthunderstorms, with the highest coverage around the White\nMountains, where some storms will contain heavy rainfall. Expect\ntoday's activity to be mainly diurnal, dissipating by around\nsunset if not before. By Thursday - the center of high pressure is\nforecast to slide to our south-southwest, resulting in the drier\nair moving a bit farther south into our area. Isolated storms will\nbe possible across the White Mountains and Yavapai/Gila counties,\nwith dry weather elsewhere. The hot temperatures will continue on\nThursday afternoon. \n\nFriday into Saturday - a passing disturbance is forecast to move\nwest to east across our area, with moisture increasing along and\nahead of it. This will bring higher shower and thunderstorm\nchances, in the 30-60% range by later Friday afternoon through\nFriday night. Storm chances fall to 10-30% by Saturday as the \ndisturbance moves to the east. Sunday through Tuesday - troughing \nin the northwest US will increase southwest flow across our \nregion, with gusty surface winds reaching 25-35 mph. The western \nedge of the moisture will also be nearby though there is some \nuncertainty this far out regarding where it will set up. This will\nallow for daily storm chances, highest in eastern AZ and tapering\noff to 10% or less in western AZ. Daytime temperatures are \nforecast to be around seasonal averages.",
